# API configuration — copy to `.env` and adjust.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is ignored by git. No real secret belongs in this file.
# Variables marked REQUIRED fail the API startup when missing (validation lives in
# apps/api/src/config/environment.ts).


# --- Runtime ------------------------------------------------------------------

# development | test | production. Default: development.
NODE_ENV=development

# Port the API listens on. Default: 3000.
# In production, Cloud Run imposes the value through this variable (ADR 0004).
PORT=3000


# --- Persistence (ADR 0006) ----------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED. Postgres connection string, `postgres://` or `postgresql://` — the startup
# refuses anything else.
# In production it is the Neon endpoint, held in Secret Manager under this same name and
# injected by Cloud Run (`infra/envs/prod/main.tf`); Terraform fills that secret from the
# Neon module rather than by hand, so the value never transits the state.
# Locally, the Postgres of the docker-compose stack — port 5433, not 5432: the stack
# publishes it there to avoid colliding with a Postgres already installed on the host.
# Never commit a real value here.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://pick_a_book:pick_a_book@localhost:5433/pick_a_book

# REQUIRED. Bucket the submitted shelf photos are kept in (ADR 0004). In production it is the
# bucket Terraform creates (`infra/envs/prod`); locally it is the one the compose stack
# emulates, seeded as the `docker/fake-gcs-data/pick-a-book-photos` directory.
BUCKET_NAME=pick-a-book-photos

# Points the bucket client at the local emulator instead of Google's API. Leave it unset in
# production: the SDK then talks to the real bucket with the ambient service account.
#
# Deliberately NOT named STORAGE_EMULATOR_HOST: the Cloud Storage SDK reads that variable by
# itself and takes it as the entire base URL, dropping the `/storage/v1` prefix the emulator
# serves — every call would 404. The SDK documents it as experimental and points at
# `apiEndpoint`, which is what the adapter passes.
BUCKET_EMULATOR_HOST=http://localhost:4443

# Owner segment of a stored photo's key (`{OWNER_ID}/shelf_photo/{id}`). Default: default.
# A fixed value, not an account: this feature introduces no authentication
# (specs/001-photo-upload/research.md §10). Laying the bucket out per owner now means real
# accounts later change where this value comes from, never what is already stored.
OWNER_ID=default


# --- Frontend (ADR 0004) -------------------------------------------------------

# Origin the API accepts browser calls from. Default: http://localhost:4200, the port
# `yarn web` serves on. The frontend is served from a bucket and the API from Cloud Run, so
# the two never share an origin and CORS is not optional.
WEB_ORIGIN=http://localhost:4200


# --- Recognition (ADR 0005) ----------------------------------------------------

# Which VLM answers a scan: gemini | qwen | stub. Default: stub.
#
# `stub` calls nothing and needs no key — it is what lets the API boot, and the frontend be
# worked on, without a key or a bill. Selecting a real provider makes ITS key required, and
# only its key: the startup fails with the name of the one that is missing.
#
# In production, set this to `gemini`: the bench measured it as the current best provider
# (docs/decisions/0001-fournisseur-vlm-par-defaut.md). The value stays `stub` here so a fresh
# copy boots with no key; switching provider is pure config, never code (ports & adapters).
SHELF_SCANNER_PROVIDER=stub

# Google AI Studio key. REQUIRED when SHELF_SCANNER_PROVIDER=gemini.
# Never commit a real value here.
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# OpenRouter key. REQUIRED when SHELF_SCANNER_PROVIDER=qwen.
# OpenRouter rather than DashScope: DashScope requires an Alibaba Cloud account with identity
# verification even on its free tier (issue #10).
# Never commit a real value here.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
